# Compile targets

@lede
Three targets, three audiences. Same source.
@end

The `brief compile` command takes a `--target=` flag. The compiler runs
the same lex / parse / validate pipeline regardless; only the emit phase
differs.

@t
| Target | Flag | Audience | Output extension
| HTML | `--target=html` | Humans, browsers, static sites | `.html`
| LLM | `--target=llm` | Language models, prompts | `.txt`
| JSON-AST | `--target=json` | Tooling, linters, transforms | `.json`

The default target is `html` unless `compile.default_target` in
`brief.toml` overrides it.

## HTML target

The bread-and-butter rendering for human readers and browsers. Emphasis
becomes `<strong>`, `<em>`, `<u>`, `<s>`. Lists become `<ul>` / `<ol>`.
Tables become semantic `<table>` markup. Custom shortcodes substitute
their `template_html`.

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=html              # to stdout
brief compile doc.brf --target=html -w           # writes doc.html
brief compile doc.brf --target=html -o out.html  # explicit path
```

The HTML target is **lossless**. Every Brief construct round-trips into
HTML; the formatter can reproduce the original source from the AST.

## LLM target

A deterministic, token-minimized rendering intended for inclusion in LLM
prompts. The transformation rules are listed in @ref[llm-mode/overview.brf](LLM mode);
the highlights:

- Comments are stripped entirely.
- Indentation collapses to one space per level.
- Heading sigils are preserved.
- Emphasis markers are preserved by default.
- JSON code blocks minify by default. Other languages minify when their
  language tag is in the configured allowlist.
- Custom shortcodes use their `template_llm`.
- Cross-page `@ref` collapses to its display text — the link target is
  meaningless in a flattened LLM context.

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm -w
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm --report-tokens
```

LLM mode is **lossy by design**. The compiler is deterministic — the same
input always produces the same byte sequence — but you cannot run the
transformation in reverse.

## JSON-AST target

The parsed document tree, dumped for inspection. Useful when you are
writing tooling that wants to see what the parser produced without
re-implementing it.

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=json -w
```

The current output is Rust's pretty-printed debug format, not
schema-stable JSON — field names mirror the AST structs, and every block
and inline node carries its source span as `start` / `len` byte offsets.
Treat the exact shape as unstable until a real serializer lands.

## Token budgeting

`--report-tokens` works on the LLM target only:

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > /dev/null
brief: doc.brf -> 1,247 chars, ~340 tokens (chars/4 heuristic)
```

The estimate is a plain characters-divided-by-four heuristic — cheap and
tokenizer-agnostic, but off by a wide margin on dense code or JSON. Treat
the number as a relative gauge between two phrasings, never as a budget
figure; for real numbers run your target model's tokenizer. See
@ref[llm-mode/token-budgeting.brf](Token budgeting) for measurement
guidance.
